{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793564242,"nonce":"B93SQCtuejYspT2XIIhQdsypyt8=","title":"ash_hdll_channel and ash_hdll_sqlite declare signatures stock HashLink rejects","message":"Found while answering puffpuff-03 about stock HashLink's signature check (hl 1.15 src/module.c compares each native's declared signature, built as 'P' + args + '_' + return, against the string the hdll's resolver reports).\n\nash_hdll_channel (natives.rs) and ash_hdll_sqlite (lib.rs) pass hl_abi's define_prim! literals such as \"biii\", \"ibiii\", \"i_\", \"b?\", \"?_\", \"?A\". These are not in hl.h's letter grammar and have no leading P, so a stock HashLink loading either hdll would stop at the first function with 'Invalid signature'. Ash never reads the string (native_lib.rs resolve_function_uncached discards `sign`), which is why nothing has failed here.\n\nash_hdll_future and ash_hdll_simd already use full P-prefixed signatures and pass on stock hl 1.15 (confirmed by puffpuff-03 for ash-future and ash-simd).\n\nDecide whether channel and sqlite are meant to run on stock HashLink. If so, rewrite their literals as full signatures and check them with stock hl; if not, say so in their READMEs.","files":null}]}